<TopicPage slug="subjects/computer-science/advanced/software-engineering-and-version-control">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Writing code is a fraction of software engineering. The discipline is about
    <strong>programs that change</strong>: written by many people, over years, on machines you'll
    never see. The answer to that reality is version control, tests, reviews and small increments
    — practices that turn solo coding into a team sport that doesn't rot.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the five practices that make software survive its authors" widget="equationStep" config={sweCfg} />

  <h2>The wisdom, compressed</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Brooks's law:</strong> adding people to a late project makes it later — communication paths grow as n(n−1)/2. Mythical Man-Month, 1975, still true.</li>
    <li><strong>Premature optimisation is the root of all evil</strong> (Knuth): make it work, make it right, make it fast — in that order, with measurements between steps.</li>
    <li><strong>Technical debt is real:</strong> every shortcut charges interest. Not all debt is bad (ship the demo!) — unpaid-forever debt is what kills projects.</li>
    <li><strong>Simple beats clever:</strong> code is read ten times more than written. Debugging is twice as hard as writing, so write for the reader.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Brooks, The Mythical Man-Month (1975); Knuth (1974); Google Engineering Practices documentation; Chacon & Straub, Pro Git.</p>
</TopicPage>
